//Virtualisation 
  Virtualization is a core technology of cloud computing.
  It allows the creation of multiple computing environments on a single physical machine.
 \These environments are called virtual because they behave like real hardware/OS but are actually simulated by software.

  hardware virtualisation most common 
   multiple os or software stacks can run on teh same physical hardware 
    eg: windows and linux on the same server 
  Virtual machines (VMs)
    Each environment runs in a VM instance.
    VMs operate in complete isolation from each other.
    Ensures security and stability: one VM crashing doesn’t affect others.
  eg: A cloud server may host several VMs: one running a web server, another running a database server, all on the same physical hardware.

//benefits 
  Resource Utilization: Makes better use of physical hardware.
  Isolation: Each VM is independent.
  Flexibility: Easy to deploy, move, or scale environments.
  Cost Efficiency: Multiple software stacks on one machine reduces hardware costs.

    | Concept                  | Explanation                                                                                                                                                                                          |
    | ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
    | **Virtualization**       | The **technology/concept** that allows creating multiple simulated computing environments on a single physical machine. It’s the **underlying idea** that makes cloud computing possible.            |
    | **Virtual Machine (VM)** | The **actual instance/application** of virtualization. A VM is a **container** or environment that runs on top of the virtualized hardware, complete with its own operating system and applications. |


// Web 2.0
  The Web is the primary interface through which cloud
computing delivers its services.
  
  Web 2.0 enables developers to architect applications and
deliver services through the Internet.
  . You don’t need to install heavy software locally; the web itself becomes the gateway to cloud computing.
  . Web 2.0 introduced interactivity and flexibility in websites
  . Developers can now build rich applications that run in a browser and communicate with servers in real-time.

  technologies behind web 2.0 


| **Enabling Technology**                 | **How It Supports Cloud Computing**                                |
| --------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **Virtualization**                      | Resource abstraction, multi-tenancy, scalability                   |
| **Service-Oriented Architecture (SOA)** | Modular services, integration, reusable APIs                       |
| **Web 2.0**                             | User-driven, interactive, collaborative cloud apps                 |
| **Browser as a Platform**               | Access cloud services anywhere, no installation needed             |
| **Parallel Processing**                 | Fast computing, large-scale task execution, efficient resource use |

